What Pydantic writes:
{
 "$defs": {
  "PaymentBasis": {
   "enum": [
    "invoice",
    "receipt"
   ],
   "title": "PaymentBasis",
   "type": "string"
  }
 },
 "properties": {
  "payment_days": {
   "description": "Days allowed to pay a valid invoice.",
   "title": "Payment Days",
   "type": "integer"
  },
  "payment_basis": {
   "$ref": "#/$defs/PaymentBasis"
  },
  "liability_cap_usd": {
   "anyOf": [
    {
     "type": "integer"
    },
    {
     "type": "null"
    }
   ],
   "description": "Flat cap in dollars, or null.",
   "title": "Liability Cap Usd"
  }
 },
 "required": [
  "payment_days",
  "payment_basis",
  "liability_cap_usd"
 ],
 "title": "Terms",
 "type": "object"
}

What strict mode sends (differences from the above):
  required:              ['payment_days', 'payment_basis', 'liability_cap_usd']
  additionalProperties:  False
  liability_cap_usd:     {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "null"}], "description": "Flat cap in dollars, or null.", "title": "Liability Cap Usd"}

Does the provider accept these keywords in a strict schema?
  minimum / maximum  accepted
  pattern            accepted
  maxItems           accepted

Whatever the answer, Pydantic enforces its own constraints on the way back in.
